L511: ONLINE SUPPLEMENTARY MATERIAL: KiwiC for Vitality Trial
L512: **Below Saturation Vitamin C Analysis Results (** _**n**_ **= 128)**
L513: Subgroup analyses were conducted on 128 people who maintained below saturation plasma vitamin C
L514: levels (<60 µmol/L) across the two lead-in blood samples (Week 0 and Week 2). There were few
L515: differences in demographic characteristics, baseline health characteristics, or baseline vitality measures
L516: between those 128 participants included in the below saturation vitamin C analyses compared to the
L517: 39 participants excluded from these analyses (using Chi-Square statistics or t-tests, all not significant),
L518: except that included participants drank less alcohol (t(165) = -2.116, _p_ = .036) and were more likely to be
L519: Asian (χ [2] (1, 167) = 6.152, p = .013) than excluded participants.
L520: Participant characteristics for this subgroup are shown in **Supplemental Table 3.** Of the 128
L521: participants, 45 were in the placebo condition, 40 were in the vitamin C condition, and 43 were in the
L522: kiwifruit condition. The three conditions were mostly equivalent in demographic characteristics,
L523: baseline health characteristics, baseline vitality measures, and baseline vitamin C levels (all ANOVAs
L524: or Chi-Squares not significant), except that participants in the kiwifruit condition were younger (F
L525: (2,125) = 6.815, _p_ = .002) than the other conditions. There were also three trends indicating that
L526: participants in the kiwifruit condition completed fewer years of study (F(2,125) = 2.920, p =.054), had
L527: lower sleep quality during the lead-in (F(2,125) = 2.631, p =.076), and were less likely to be Asian
L528: (χ [2] (2,128) = 5.95, p = .051) than the other conditions. The changes in vitamin C levels for this subgroup
L529: are shown in **Supplemental Table 4.**
L530: The changes in the vitality outcomes by condition for the below saturation vitamin C analyses ( _n_ = 128)
L531: are shown in **Supplemental Table 5** and **Supplemental Figure 1** . For the POMS total mood
L532: disturbance, only participants in the kiwifruit condition ( **Supplemental Figure 1C** ) showed
L533: significantly reduced total mood disturbance after two weeks of intervention (Study Week 4), but not
L534: at the end of the intervention (Study Week 6) or Washout (Study Week 8). Participants in the vitamin
L535: C condition showed non-significant reductions in their POMS total mood disturbance ( **Supplemental**
L536: **Figure 1B** ) and those in the placebo condition were unchanged over time ( **Supplemental Figure 1A** ).
L537: Participants in both the vitamin C and kiwifruit conditions showed a reduction in fatigue, either after
L538: two weeks of supplementation ( **Supplemental Figure 1F** ), or four weeks of supplementation
L539: ( **Supplemental Figure 1E** ). Fatigue scores returned to baseline levels at washout for both conditions.
L540: No changes were seen in the placebo group. For well-being, there was a significant increase in well
L541: being after a week of kiwifruit supplementation, which stayed elevated at washout ( **Supplemental**
L542: **Figure 1I** ). Participants in the vitamin C group showed an increase in well-being after four weeks of
L543: intervention ( **Supplemental Figure 1H** ), which was eliminated at washout. There was no change in
L544: well-being throughout the study in the placebo group except for a trend decrease after two weeks of
L545: placebo supplementation ( **Supplemental Figure 1G** ).
L546: Analysis of variance ( **Table 5**, main manuscript) methods showed significant condition x time effects when
L547: comparing Study Week 2 (end of lead-in) with Study Week 4 (second week of intervention) for POMS total
L548: mood disturbance (F(2, 125) = 3.167, _p_ = .046, ηp [2] = 0.048), fatigue (F(2, 125) = 3.563, _p_ = .031, ηp [2 ] = 0.054), and
L549: well-being (F(2, 125) = 6.177, _p_ = .003, ηp [2] = 0.090). When comparing Study Week 2 (end of lead-in) with
L550: Study Week 6 (end of intervention), there was a trend condition x time effect for fatigue (F(2,125) = 2.993, p
L551: =.054, ηp [2] = 0.046) but not for POMS total mood disturbance (F(2,125) = 1.919, _p_ =.151, ηp [2] = 0.030) or well
L552: being (F(2,125) = 1.995, _p_ = .140, ηp [2] = 0.031). When comparing Study Week 2, Week 4, and Week 6, condition
L553: ONLINE SUPPLEMENTARY MATERIAL: KiwiC for Vitality Trial
L554: x time effects were significant for fatigue (F(2,125) = 3.175, _p_ = .014, ηp [2] = 0.048) and well-being (F(2,125) =
L555: 3.514, _p_ = .008, ηp [2] = 0.053) but not for POMS total mood disturbance (F(2,125) = 1.944, _p_ = .104, ηp [2] = 0.030).
L556: The intervention effects in the below saturation vitamin C analyses ( _n_ = 128) were not due to differences
L557: between conditions in sleep quantity (intervention weeks 3-4, F(2,125) = 0.086, _p_ = .918; intervention
L558: weeks 5-6, F(2,125) = 0.677, _p_ = .510), sleep quality (intervention weeks 3-4, F(2,125) = 0.900, _p_ = .409;
L559: intervention weeks 5-6, F(2,125) = 0.861, _p_ = .425), physical activity (intervention weeks 3-4, F(2,125) =
L560: 0.655, _p_ = .521; intervention weeks 5-6, F(2,125) = 0.155, _p_ = .856), BMI at end of intervention ( _Welch’s_
L561: _F_ (2,124) = 79.653, _p_ = .932), or dietary factors such as total energy intake (KJ) ( _Welch’s F_ (2,82.375) = 2.153,
L562: _p_ = .123), or total protein intake ( _Welch’s F_ (2,78.609) = 0.030, _p_ = .970). There was a significant difference
L563: between conditions for total fat intake ( _Welch’s F_ (2,80.868) = 4.326, _p_ = .016). A post-hoc Games-Howell
L564: test indicated significantly lower total fat intake (grams) for the kiwifruit condition during the second
L565: half of the intervention (weeks 5 – 6) compared to the vitamin C condition ( _p_ = .016; **Supplemental**
L566: **Table 6** ). There was also a trend for total carbohydrate intake ( _F_ (2,125) = 2.370, _p_ = .098), driven by
L567: participants in the vitamin C condition consuming slightly more carbohydrates than participants in the
L568: placebo condition ( _p_ = .098).
L569: As shown in the ANCOVA results in **Table 5** (main manuscript), most of the effects remained
L570: significant when adjusting for age, Asian ethnicity, year at university or polytechnic, lead-in sleep
L571: quality (weeks 1-2), fat intake during intervention (weeks 5-6), and carbohydrate intake during
L572: intervention (weeks 5-6). The condition x time effects comparing Week 2 and Week 4 were still
L573: significant for fatigue (F(2,119) = 3.514, _p_ = .033, ηp [2] = 0.056) and well-being (F(2,119) = 5.012, _p_ = .008, ηp [2]
L574: = 0.078) and were attenuated for POMS total mood disturbance (F(2,119) = 2.729, _p_ = .069, ηp [2] = 0.044).
L575: There were no significant condition x time effects when comparing Study Week 2 (end of lead-in) with
L576: Study Week 6 (end of intervention) except for a trend for fatigue (fatigue F(2,119) = 2.842, _p_ = .062, ηp [2 ] =
L577: 0.046; POMS total mood disturbance F(2, 119) = 1.830, _p_ = .165, ηp [2] = 0.030; well-being (F(2,119) = 1.989,
L578: _p_ = .141, ηp [2] = 0.032). Condition x time effects comparing Week 2, Week 4, and Week 6 were still
L579: significant for fatigue (F(2, 119) = 2.793, _p_ = .027, ηp [2] = 0.045) and well-being (F(2,119) = 2.834, _p_ = .025,
L580: ηp [2] = 0.045) but not for POMS total mood disturbance (F(2, 119) = 1.634, _p_ = .166, ηp [2] = 0.027).
