L696: ONLINE SUPPLEMENTARY MATERIAL: KiwiC for Vitality Trial
L697: **Per Protocol Analysis Results (** _**n**_ **= 92)**
L698: Subgroup per protocol (PP) analyses were conducted on 92 people who maintained a vitamin C level
L699: <40 µmol/L from screening until the beginning of the study (Week 0). There were few differences in
L700: demographic characteristics, baseline health characteristics, or baseline vitality measures between those
L701: 92 participants included in the PP analyses compared to the 75 participants excluded from these
L702: analyses (using Chi-Square statistics or t-tests, all not significant), except that the 92 included
L703: participants had higher baseline sleep quality (t(164) = 2.151, _p_ = .033) and were more likely to be Asian
L704: (trend only) (χ [2] (1, 167) = 3.736, _p_ = .053) than excluded participants.
L705: Participant characteristics for this subgroup are shown in **Supplemental Table 7** . Of the 92 participants,
L706: 36 were in the placebo condition, 29 were in the vitamin C condition, and 27 were in the kiwifruit
L707: condition. The three conditions were mostly equivalent in demographic characteristics, baseline health
L708: characteristics, baseline vitality measures, and baseline vitamin C levels (all ANOVAs or Chi-Squares
L709: not significant). However, participants in the kiwifruit condition had younger participants (F(2,89) =
L710: 5.757, _p_ = .004) who had completed fewer years of study (F(2,89) = 3.215, _p_ = .045) than the other
L711: conditions. Plasma vitamin C concentrations during the study for PP participants are shown in
L712: **Supplemental Table 8** . Participants in the vitamin C and kiwifruit conditions achieved saturation in
L713: vitamin C after two weeks of supplementation (Week 4, Intervention).
L714: The changes in the vitality outcomes by condition for the per protocol analyses ( _n_ = 92) are shown in
L715: **Supplemental Table 9** and **Supplemental Figure 2** . For the POMS total mood disturbance, participants
L716: in the vitamin C condition ( **Supplemental Figure 2B** ) showed significantly reduced total mood
L717: disturbance after two weeks of intervention (Study Week 4), but not at the end of the intervention
L718: (Study Week 6) or Washout (Study Week 8). Participants in the kiwifruit condition showed no
L719: significant reduction in their POMS total mood disturbance ( **Supplemental Figure 2C** ) and those in the
L720: placebo condition had a slight increase in total mood disturbance at washout in comparison to the end
L721: of lead-in ( **Supplemental Figure 2A** ). Participants in the vitamin C condition showed a significant
L722: reduction in fatigue after two weeks and a trend decrease in fatigue after four weeks of
L723: supplementation ( **Supplemental Figure 2E** ). For the kiwifruit condition, there was a non-significant
L724: reduction in fatigue after two weeks that returned to prior levels at four weeks ( **Supplemental Figure**
L725: **2F).** In the placebo condition, there was a trend increase in fatigue at washout when compared to the
L726: end of lead-in ( **Supplemental Figure 2D)** . For well-being, there was a significant increase in well-being
L727: after two weeks of kiwifruit supplementation, which did not decrease at washout ( **Supplemental**
L728: **Figure 2I** ). Participants in the vitamin C group also showed a trend increase in well-being at the end of
L729: the intervention ( **Supplemental Figure 2H** ), which was eliminated at washout. There was no change
L730: in well-being throughout the study in the placebo group ( **Supplemental Figure 2G** ).
L731: Analysis of variance ( **Table 5**, main manuscript) showed significant condition x time effects when
L732: comparing Study Week 2 (end of lead-in) with Study Week 4 (second week of intervention) for POMS
L733: total mood disturbance (F(2, 89) = 3.841, _p_ = .025, ηp [2] = 0.079), fatigue (F(2, 89) = 3.621, _p_ = .031, ηp [2 ] =
L734: 0.075), and well-being (F(2, 189) = 3.611, _p_ = .031, ηp [2] = 0.075). When comparing Study Week 2 (end of
L735: lead-in) with Study Week 6 (end of intervention), there was a significant effect for fatigue (F(2,89) =
L736: 3.113, _p_ = .049, ηp [2] = 0.065), but not POMS total mood disturbance (F(2,89) = 2.099, _p_ = .129, ηp [2] = 0.045)
L737: ONLINE SUPPLEMENTARY MATERIAL: KiwiC for Vitality Trial
L738: or well-being (F(2,89) = 1.700, _p_ = .189, ηp [2] = 0.037). When comparing Study Week 2, Week 4, and Week
L739: 6, the condition x time effects were significant for fatigue (F(2,89) = 3.021, _p_ = .019, ηp [2] = 0.064) and
L740: showed trends for POMS total mood disturbance (F(2,89) = 2.262, _p_ = .064, ηp [2] = 0.048) and well-being
L741: (F(2,89) = 2.088, _p_ = .084, ηp [2] = 0.045).
L742: The intervention effects in the PP analyses were not due to differences between conditions in sleep
L743: quantity (intervention weeks 3-4, F(2,89) = 0.061, _p_ = .941; intervention weeks 5-6, F(2,89) = 0.590, _p_ =
L744: .557), sleep quality (intervention weeks 3-4, F(2,89) = 1.293, _p_ = .279; intervention weeks 5-6, F(2,89) =
L745: 1.203, _p_ = .305), physical activity intervention weeks 5-6, F(2,89) = 0.315, _p_ = .731), BMI ( _Welch’s_
L746: _F_ (2,58.479) = 0.484, _p_ = .619), or dietary factors such as total energy intake (KJ) (F(2,89) = 1.537, _p_ = .221),
L747: total protein intake ( _Welch’s F_ (2,55.251) = 0.292, _p_ = .748), or total fat intake ( _F_ (2,89) = 1.115, _p_ = .332).
L748: There was a significant difference between conditions for total carbohydrate intake ( _F_ (2,89) = 3.246, _p_ =
L749: .044). A post-hoc Tukey test indicated significantly lower total carbohydrate intake (grams) for the
L750: placebo condition during the second half of the intervention (weeks 5 – 6) compared to the vitamin C
L751: condition ( _p_ = .034; **Supplemental Table 10** ). There was also a trend difference in physical activity levels
L752: (intervention weeks 3-4, F(2,89) = 2.419, _p_ = .095) driving by a participants in the vitamin C condition
L753: engaging in slightly more days with physical activity compared to the kiwifruit condition ( _p_ =.099;
L754: **Supplemental Table 9** ).
L755: When adjusting for age, year at university or polytechnic, physical activity during intervention (weeks
L756: 3-4), and carbohydrate intake during intervention (weeks 5-6), condition x time effects comparing Week
L757: 2 (Lead-in) and Week 4 (second week of intervention) remained significant for well-being (F(2,85)
L758: =3.590, _p_ = .032, ηp [2] = 0.078) and were attenuated for POMS total mood disturbance (F(2,85) = 2.874, _p_ =
L759: .062, ηp [2] = 0.063) and fatigue (F(2,85) = 2.992, _p_ = .056, ηp [2] = 0.066). Results were attenuated for condition
L760: x time effects when comparing Study Week 2 (end of lead-in) with Study Week 6 (end of intervention)
L761: for fatigue (fatigue F(2,85) = 2.554, _p_ = .084, ηp [2 ] = 0.057) and not significant for POMS total mood
L762: disturbance (F(2,85) = 1.270, _p_ = .286, ηp [2] = 0.029) and well-being (F(2,85) = 1.463, _p_ = .237, ηp [2] = 0.033).
L763: When comparing Week 2, Week 4, and Week 6, condition x time effects were significant for fatigue
L764: (F(2,85) = 2.638, _p_ = .036, ηp [2] = 0.058) and attenuated for POMS total mood disturbance (F(2,85) = 1.635,
L765: _p_ = .168, ηp [2] = 0.037) and well-being (F(2,85) = 1.981, _p_ = .100, ηp [2] = 0.045). See **Table 5** in the main
L766: manuscript for full ANCOVA results.
