L884: **Sensitivity Analysis using Pattern Mixture Models**
L885: Pattern mixture models were used to test the robustness of assumptions for the primary analysis,
L886: assessing the plausible impact of data missing not at random (MNAR). Patterns in a pattern mixture
L887: model represent different groups of missing data. It is thought participants who drop out of a study
L888: may have a different distribution from those who remain in the study and have observed data. Different
L889: patterns of missing data can be classified by the time point in which participants dropped out, by
L890: participant reasons for dropping out, or auxiliary variables collected at baseline. We do acknowledge
L891: that this type of sensitivity analysis cannot assess all possibilities for missing data, as it is limited by
L892: possible under-identification of subgroups that can explain the missing data, as data required to
L893: determine these subgroups may be missing. However, by definition, these possible subgroups are
L894: untestable. A strength of the current study is that at baseline we collected data on several auxiliary
L895: variables, such as age, gender, ethnicity, smoking status, year of study, typical weekly alcohol
L896: consumption, weight, fruit and vegetable consumption, and socioeconomic status, which were used to
L897: determine differences between participants with observed and missing data on primary outcomes.
L898: For the primary outcomes, missing data followed a monotone pattern. If data was missing at a time
L899: point (k), then data would also be missing at the following time point(s) (k+1). As such, a pattern
L900: mixture approach accounting for the probability of drop out over time was taken ( **Supplemental Table**
L901: **11** ). Recommendations and equations were taken from the panel of handling missing data in clinical
L902: trials for monotone missing data; National Research Council, 2010.
L903: **Supplemental Table 11.** Discontinued participants (cumulative percentage ( _n_ ))
