L937: Where 𝜇𝜇𝑘𝑘∗ = 𝜇𝜇𝑘𝑘 + ∆𝜇 and 𝛽𝛽𝑘𝑘∗ = 𝛽𝛽𝑘𝑘 + ∆𝛽 . The ∆𝜇 being the difference between the mean of 𝑌𝑌𝑘𝑘 for
L938: individuals who drop out at k-1 and individuals remained in the study at k-1. The same principle can
L939: be used to determine ∆𝛽, however, to simplify the number of sensitivity parameters, ∆𝛽 = 0, assuming
L940: that ∆𝜇 is similar between patterns.
L941: There was no significant difference between participants who remained in the study and participants
L942: who dropped out for age, gender, smoking status, typical weekly alcohol consumption, weight, fruit
L943: and vegetable consumption, or socioeconomic status. However, there were differences in ethnicity, in
L944: which more non-Asian participants dropped out than Asian participants and participants who had
L945: completed fewer years of study were more likely to drop out. **Supplemental Table 12** shows the
L946: ANOVA results for sensitivity analysis under different patterns of missingness. Pattern one assumes
L947: that data is missing at random (MAR, Δ=0), considering drop out over time. Pattern two assumes that
L948: ONLINE SUPPLEMENTARY MATERIAL: KiwiC for Vitality Trial
L949: there are differences between conditions for participants who drop out over time. Pattern three grouped
L950: participants who dropped out based on their vitamin C levels into four groups; unhealthy levels of
L951: vitamin C (<23µmol/L), adequate vitamin C, (23-40µmol/L), healthy vitamin C (41-60µmol/L), and
L952: saturated (>60µmol/L), conditional on condition and time of drop out. Pattern four grouped
L953: participants based on their reason for withdrawal or dropout over time. Groupings based on reasons
L954: for drop out included medical or health reasons, no contact / no show, and disliked assigned condition
L955: and unwilling to adhere. Pattern five addresses the ethnicity discrepancies between conditions over
L956: time. Pattern six addresses the year of study differences between participants who dropped out and
L957: participants who completed the study.
L958: Sensitivity analysis ( **Supplemental Table 12** ) overall indicated that missing data was mostly negligible,
L959: and analyses were robust as the patterns of results remained the same or were marginally strengthened
L960: with the increased power. Sensitivity analysis for patterns one, two, three, four, and six gave
L961: comparable results to the primary analysis for all vitality outcomes, although they were slightly
L962: strengthened. Pattern five results were strengthened for total mood disturbance, fatigue, and wellbeing, which may suggest that there is an ethnicity-linked difference between conditions. Ethnicitylinked differences between vitamin C levels and vitality outcomes have also been noted in the main
L963: analysis and may require further investigation. However, our study was under-powered to fully
L964: explore such effects.
